virglrenderer: clip distances and a last tessellation evaluation shader without cull distances

What: on a desktop GL without cull distances (Apple's GL 4.1 has no ARB_cull_distance) vrend
writes clip distances. A vertex, tessellation or geometry shader that writes gl_ClipDistance
compiles and clips; user clip planes (glClipPlane in a compatibility context) clip after the
last vertex, tessellation evaluation or geometry shader. A tessellation evaluation shader that
is the last stage before the fragment shader gets the y prescale and its transform feedback
stores, as a vertex shader does. gl_CullDistance is still never declared there: a shader that
writes one stays refused, as before.
Why: vrend took "has cull distances" for "has clip distances". gl_ClipDistance is core in
desktop GLSL 1.30; only GLES needs EXT_clip_cull_distance, which the cull distance feature
stands for there. On the Mac a shader that wrote gl_ClipDistance did not compile (it wrote an
undeclared clip_dist_temp) and its draws were skipped; clip planes and the clip distances of
tessellation and geometry shaders were ignored. The tessellation evaluation shader's end sat
inside the same test: under a flipped viewport (a guest's window) tessellated geometry landed
upside down, and its transform feedback recorded zeros and left GL error 0x502 for the next
command (#589).
How: has_clip_distance() (cull distances, or desktop GL) gates the clip declarations and
stores of all four stages. The user clip planes' stores sit in a shader variant of their own:
the geometry shader's gs.emit_clip_distance as before, and a new key bit ucp_enabled for the
last vertex or tessellation evaluation shader, set only on a desktop GL without cull
distances. A shader drawn without clip planes keeps its GLSL byte for byte; programs drawn
with planes on and off get one more variant. A last tessellation evaluation shader now uses
vrend's VirglBlock (one uniform block binding), as the vertex and geometry shaders do. A
loose gl_ClipDistance only appears in a last stage that is not separable and a gl_PerVertex
block only in a stage that is not last or in a separable one, so the two never meet (that
crashed Apple's GLSL compiler, virgl-567). The clip distance members that
virgl-shader-separable-pervertex.patch puts into a separable stage's block now apply on the
Mac too.
Upstream: the same gates in virglrenderer main (e0a1caf3, checked 2026-10-31); not sent.
Test: Tests/virgl/test-whole-program-shapes.c rows A, B and C (12 fail without this patch,
also with planes switched on and off for one program), Tests/virgl/test-core-glsl-shaders.c
(exact texts unchanged).

